Date: Fri, 28 Aug 2026 21:14:40 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 10193/11362] REQ-CONDUCTOR-VERDICT-3: choose verdict_class by
 the RUN, not the RESULT

REQ-CONDUCTOR-VERDICT-2 defines what each enum member does to a CONSUMER and
never defines what it means to a WRITER. That gap is measurable: of 73
artifacts declaring a verdict_class, 9 declare `partial` and 7 of those open
their honest_verdict with a terminal prefix -- a completed run describing a
mixed result. Six are capstones.

The collision is in the word. To the consumer `partial` means THE RUN MAY
RETRY. To those seven writers it meant THE EVIDENCE IS MIXED. Both readings are
natural; only one is implemented. exp6687 ran 2,983 seconds, produced a
complete four-branch synthesis, and was logged FAIL
artifact_not_updated_past_bootstrap -- a message asserting the agent bailed
without writing anything -- then queued for re-run until it hit the three-strike
skip.

The rule now states, per member, what the WRITER should declare, keyed on
whether the run finished rather than on whether the result was positive. A run
that attempted everything it planned and found a mixed or absent effect is
`null`: an honest negative, a finding, and re-running it changes nothing.
`partial` is reserved for a run that genuinely stopped early.

NO CONSUMER CHANGE, deliberately. SCENARIO-CONDUCTOR-VERDICT-5 stands: a
declared `partial` stays untrustworthy even behind a `complete:` prefix,
because a truncated run must not launder itself through free text an agent
authored. I tried exactly that fix this morning and
test_partial_declaration_beats_terminal_prefix caught it -- the control is
right and I was wrong. This requirement removes the incentive to try by giving
the mixed-result case a correct member to declare, instead of weakening the
check that protects the other case.

The seven are named and NOT auto-corrected. Each needs its WRITER changed --
exp6687's own validator asserts verdict_class != "partial" is an error, so
correcting the artifact alone would be recreated on the next run, which is the
exp3946 lesson -- and each needs a human judgement about whether `null` or
`blocked` fits its evidence. A sweep would be the wrong tool.

